\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Graph energy and the constant modes}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring, #{V,E,s,h} finite incidence
data and #{w:E\to R} arbitrary weights. With the counting forms and
\vocabk{weighted graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007}
#{L=-\extd_{R}^{\dagger} W\extd_{R}}, one has
##{L^\dagger=L,\qquad
 b_V(f,(-L)g)=\sum_e w(e)(\extd_{R}f)(e)(\extd_{R}g)(e).}
The kernel of #{\extd_{R}} consists exactly of fields constant on each
connected component of the underlying undirected graph, and
#{\ker \extd_{R}\subseteq\ker L}. Two vertices are in the same component
when a finite path joins them, with edge orientation ignored.
The energy and Laplacian are unchanged by edge-orientation reversal.}

\proof{
\p{The weight map is self-adjoint by
\ref{fdip-00JD}. The adjoint laws give
#{L^\dagger=-\extd_{R}^{\dagger} W^\dagger \extd_{R}=L}, and the defining identity gives
##{\begin{aligned}
b_V(f,(-L)g)&=b_E(\extd_{R}f,W\extd_{R}g)\\
 &=\sum_e w(e)(\extd_{R}f)(e)(\extd_{R}g)(e).
\end{aligned}}
In particular, #{b_V(f,(-L)f)=\sum_e w(e)(\extd_{R}f)(e)^2}. Each edge
occurs once and no factor #{1/2} is used. The equality #{\extd_{R}f=0}
says that endpoint values agree along every edge, hence along every
path. Conversely, a componentwise constant field has zero difference
on each edge. This identifies #{\ker \extd_{R}} with the function module
on the components. Such a field also satisfies #{Lf=-\extd_{R}^{\dagger} W0=0}.}
\p{Reversing an edge negates both factors in its energy summand.
The Laplacian itself is unchanged by the sign-map identity in
\ref{fdip-00JD}.}
}

\p{Now specialize to #{R=\mathbb R} and #{w(e)>0}. The real incidence map is
#{\extd_{\mathbb R}}. The Laplacian is self-adjoint. The operator #{-L} is positive
semidefinite, and
##{\ker L=\ker\extd_{\mathbb R},\qquad \dim\ker L=k,}
where #{k} is the number of connected components.
If #{V} is nonempty and connected, the kernel is the one-dimensional
space spanned by the constant field #{\mathbf1_V}. Its positive unit
mode is #{|V|^{-1/2}\mathbf1_V}. An isolated vertex contributes one
component and one kernel basis vector. If #{V} is empty, both the
component count and kernel dimension are zero. Loops contribute zero
energy, while distinct parallel edges each contribute their own
positive-weight summand.}

\proof{
\p{The energy identity specializes to
##{\langle f,(-L)f\rangle_V=\sum_e w(e)(\extd_{\mathbb R}f)(e)^2\ge0.}
If #{Lf=0}, this finite sum of nonnegative real terms is zero.
Every summand is therefore zero; strict positivity of #{w(e)} gives
#{\extd_{\mathbb R}f(e)=0}. The reverse inclusion was proved over #{R}. Indicators
of the nonempty components span the componentwise constant fields.
In a linear relation between them, evaluation at a vertex of any one
component makes its coefficient zero. Thus they are a basis, proving
the dimension statement. For a nonempty connected graph the one
indicator is #{\mathbf1_V}, of squared counting norm #{|V|}.
For #{V=\varnothing}, the endpoint maps force #{E=\varnothing}.
A loop has zero endpoint difference, and each parallel edge retains
its own term in the sum. An action defined as half this
[quadratic form](ca-0004) uses a separate convention.}
}

\p{More generally, for real nonnegative weights #{w(e)\ge0}, the
kernel of #{L} consists exactly of fields constant on each connected
component of the graph with vertex set #{V} and edge set
#{E_+=\{e\in E:w(e)>0\}}.}

\proof{
\p{If #{Lf=0}, the energy sum is zero and every positive-weight
edge has #{\extd_{\mathbb R}f(e)=0}. Conversely, if those endpoint differences vanish,
then #{W\extd_{\mathbb R}f=0}: positive-weight edges have zero difference and all other
edges have zero weight. Hence #{Lf=-\extd_{\mathbb R}^{\dagger}W\extd_{\mathbb R}f=0}. The path argument
already proved identifies the fields with zero difference on #{E_+}
as exactly the componentwise constant fields for that graph.}
}

\p{The energy convention and connected positive-real argument appear
in \citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective}; the ring
identity and the disconnected, loop and parallel-edge extensions are
proved above. The [four-cycle calculation](fdip-004B) displays the
energy and its coordinate Laplacian. The weight and connectivity
examples in \ref{fdip-00JF} show how the kernel and
positivity change when the hypotheses change.}
